@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix :       <#> .

<> a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "HowTo: Credential-File Location Resolution at Session Start"@en ;
    schema:description "Companion to preferences.ttl Step 209. Identity establishment and credential-file location are session-start facts, resolved once and recorded in core.ttl, not rediscovered ad hoc mid-task."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:step :stepWhatHappened, :stepFix, :stepScopeGoingForward ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <../preferences.ttl#step-credentialLocationAtSessionStart>,
        <../core.ttl>,
        <verified-identity.ttl>,
        <p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl> .

:stepWhatHappened a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    schema:name "What happened"@en ;
    schema:text """Asked to sign generated artifacts (nlp-judgment-layer-claude_sonnet_5-1.{html,md,ttl}) using the user's private key. preferences.ttl referenced the cert bundle only via an unresolved {CREDENTIALS_ROOT} placeholder (Step 143's certificate-paths note). An initial scoped find under a guessed root (~/Documents/...) found nothing, forcing a broader ad hoc filesystem search mid-task before the real path — /Users/kidehen/Documents/Management/Marketing/WebID/Templates/YouID/link-in-bio-credentials-5/cert.p12 — turned up. The user then stated: "Establishing my identity and location of my credentials file is a crucial aspect of each session initialization process" — i.e. this should never have been a mid-task scramble; it belongs in the same bucket as user-identity resolution, which the mandatory retrieval sequence already treats as a Step 1 concern (AGENTS.md: "Read core.ttl for user identity and output-routing requirements")."""@en .

:stepFix a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    schema:name "Fix applied"@en ;
    schema:text """Added a "User Credentials" section to core.ttl, immediately after the existing User Identity block, with three schema:PropertyValue entries: :credentialsRoot (the resolved {CREDENTIALS_ROOT} value, replacing the placeholder used everywhere else in preferences.ttl), :userPrincipalCredentials (the link-in-bio-credentials-5 bundle path plus its WebID SAN and owl:sameAs to the canonical identity), and :agentDelegateCredentials (the agent's own delegated bundle path). Because core.ttl is read at Step 1 of the mandatory retrieval sequence in every session (per AGENTS.md), this makes credential-file location a session-start fact resolved alongside identity, not a discovery task triggered by the first mTLS/signing/whoami request. The passphrase itself is deliberately NOT recorded anywhere in memory — core.ttl's entry points to the existing p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl convention (a local KEY=VALUE file read at unlock time, never printed/logged/persisted) rather than duplicating or weakening that rule."""@en .

:stepScopeGoingForward a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    schema:name "What this does and does not change"@en ;
    schema:text """Does change: any future task needing the user's or agent's PKCS#12 bundle path (signing, mTLS requests via mtls-curl, whoami verification, WebID-TLS delegation work) reads core.ttl's :userPrincipalCredentials / :agentDelegateCredentials values directly instead of running find. Does NOT change: the passphrase-handling rule (still read from a local KEY=VALUE file per p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl, still never stored in memory), and does not pre-authorize any signing/mTLS operation — those still require the user's explicit request and, per the global safety rules, are never performed by entering credentials into third-party surfaces; this only removes the unnecessary rediscovery step for information that was already effectively static."""@en .
